WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.920
Augusta Ada Lovelace feared she&nbsp;
had inherited her father’s madness.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:09.240
She was named after her dad’s&nbsp;
half-sister with whom he had an affair!

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.820
When Ada’s mother, Annabella Milbanke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:15.060
suspected her husband was involved&nbsp;
in an incestuous relationship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:19.740
she rejected her daughter’s first name and,&nbsp;
henceforth, called her by her middle name.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:23.520
Ada’s father was the great&nbsp;
British poet Lord Byron.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:27.540
He was as famous for his playboy&nbsp;
life as he was for his poetry.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:33.000
He had numerous affairs, including with&nbsp;
Lady Caroline Lamb, who described him as:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.280
'Mad, bad, and dangerous to know”.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:36.480
And he was.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:41.280
Five weeks after the birth of&nbsp;
Ada, Lady Byron left Lord Byron.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.980
Lord Byron wrote about seeing his daughter for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:47.700
last time in his famous poem&nbsp;
Childe Harold’s Pilgrimage:

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:53.280
When last I saw thy young blue eyes they smiled,
And then we parted,

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.320
Ada never saw her father again.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.140
When she was eight, he caught a terrible cold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:03.240
which worsened after he allowed his doctors to&nbsp;
purge his blood even though he was against it.

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:05.880
Shortly after, Lord Byron died.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:12.060
His final words were said to be: “Oh, my&nbsp;
poor dear child! - my dear Ada! My God,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:14.640
could I have seen her! Give her my blessing.”

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:18.780
There was a portrait of Lord Byron&nbsp;
at Ada’s home, but her mother kept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:23.160
it hidden behind a green curtain, and Ada&nbsp;
never saw it until she was 20 years old.

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:27.840
Apparently, it was to protect it from smoke&nbsp;
rather than because of Byron’s reputation.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:32.160
But Lady Byron wanted to make sure&nbsp;
Ada didn’t turn out like her father.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:36.600
So, she decided to have Ada&nbsp;
rigorously tutored in mathematics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:39.480
thinking it would be the best&nbsp;
antidote to her rebelliousness.

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:43.560
That was unusual for a woman in&nbsp;
the 19th century. Then again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:48.300
Ada’s mother had herself been tutored in math&nbsp;
by a former professor of Cambridge University.

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:54.300
Despite Lady Byron’s efforts to ensure Ada didn’t&nbsp;
follow the same wayward path as her father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:59.160
Ada ended up having an affair as&nbsp;
a teen with one of her tutors and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:01.320
then tried to run away from home to be with him.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:05.220
Lord Byron never wanted his&nbsp;
daughter to turn out like him.

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:10.320
He once wrote a letter to his half-sister Augusta&nbsp;
to see if she could find out what Ada was like.

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:15.600
Byron wrote: “I hope the Gods have&nbsp;
made her anything save poetical - it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.640
is enough to have one such fool in the family.”

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:21.600
Ada loved poetry. But she also loved math.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:24.360
She felt it was the only language that can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:27.300
“adequately express the great&nbsp;
facts of the natural world…”

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:30.180
And it is “the instrument&nbsp;
through which the weak mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:33.960
of man can most effectually&nbsp;
read his Creator’s works.”

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:36.720
After the scandalous affair with her tutor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:40.380
she decided to get her act together&nbsp;
and declared to her new tutor:

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:44.820
“I must cease to think of living for&nbsp;
pleasure or self-gratification. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:49.080
find that nothing but very close and&nbsp;
intense application to subjects of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:53.220
scientific nature now seems to keep&nbsp;
my imagination from running wild…”

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:57.360
It was the perfect time to immerse&nbsp;
herself in science and technology.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:03.060
Ada was born in 1815, at the height of&nbsp;
the industrial revolution in Britain.

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:06.720
She was fascinated by the factories&nbsp;
popping up in Central England.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:10.800
This was in contrast to her father,&nbsp;
who defended the British textile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:15.000
workers who felt threatened by the new&nbsp;
technology and destroyed the machinery.

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:21.720
Ada was particularly impressed by an automated&nbsp;
machine that wove patterns on fabric.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:25.920
The Jacquard Loom used punched&nbsp;
cards to create designs.

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:30.300
The design is translated into a&nbsp;
pattern of holes punched into cards.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:33.000
The cards direct the pattern of the weave,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:38.940
automating the creation of complex designs.&nbsp;
This transformed textile manufacturing.

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:45.420
In the future, punch cards would also control&nbsp;
a sequence of operations in early computers.

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:49.620
One of Britain’s few noted female&nbsp;
mathematicians took Ada under her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:53.760
wing. Mary Somerville became&nbsp;
a friend, teacher, and mentor.

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:57.180
Somerville introduced her to&nbsp;
Charles Babbage, an esteemed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:01.680
English mathematician who had an interest&nbsp;
in machines that could perform human tasks.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:07.440
Ada grew fascinated by Babbage’s idea to&nbsp;
build an “Analytical Engine”, a mechanical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:12.420
computer that would use a series of gears and&nbsp;
wheels to perform mathematical calculations.

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:16.440
In Babbage’s mind, it could&nbsp;
perform any kind of calculation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:19.260
based on the programming instructions given to it.

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:24.420
This was more advanced than his Difference&nbsp;
Engine, which was like a big basic mechanical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:28.980
calculator that could only perform a&nbsp;
specific kind of calculation, while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:33.720
the analytical engine was designed to perform&nbsp;
a wider range of mathematical calculations.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:38.400
In the way that the punch cards directed&nbsp;
the loom to weave designs on fabric,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:41.220
punch cards would program the computing machine.

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:47.040
Lovelace made the connection between the two,&nbsp;
writing this beautiful description: “...the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:53.760
Analytical Engine weaves algebraic patterns just&nbsp;
as the Jacquard loom weaves flowers and leaves.”

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:57.420
Poetry flowed through her veins,&nbsp;
as it did through her father’s.

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:01.320
She was comfortable in both&nbsp;
worlds, the arts and the sciences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:05.520
which allowed her to see the beauty in&nbsp;
the mechanics of a computing machine.

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:09.120
Ada published a description&nbsp;
of Babbage’s analytical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:15.540
engine in a British science journal in 1843.
 
This was partly a translation of a French work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:19.620
though her own additional notes ended&nbsp;
up being twice as long as the original!

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:24.660
She gave step-by-step instructions on how&nbsp;
the machine could calculate a sequence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:30.060
of Bernoulli numbers, which are mathematical&nbsp;
constants used in many areas of mathematics.

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:33.420
This is considered to be the&nbsp;
first published algorithm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:37.620
which is why she’s often referred to as&nbsp;
the world’s first computer programmer.

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:43.200
Babbage saw his Analytical Engine as primarily&nbsp;
a machine that crunched numbers. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:45.360
Ada felt it could be so much more.

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:48.540
She saw it as a general-purpose machine that could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:51.240
process anything that could&nbsp;
be written using symbols.

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:56.520
She believed it could create poetry and&nbsp;
make music, writing: “the engine might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.520 --> 00:06:01.320
compose elaborate and scientific pieces&nbsp;
of music of any degree of complexity.”

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:08.460
She imagined the world’s first computer 100 years&nbsp;
before the first ones were built in World War two.

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:11.940
There was one other interesting&nbsp;
concept in her scientific paper.

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:15.600
She raised the idea of&nbsp;
whether machines could think.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:17.700
Ada believed they could not.

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:21.960
She felt a machine could perform&nbsp;
operations as instructed, but “has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:24.720
no pretensions whatever to originate anything.”

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:30.120
British mathematician Alan Turing&nbsp;
refuted this a century later. He was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:33.660
famous for cracking the Nazi machine&nbsp;
that encrypted top-secret messages.

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:38.700
Turing’s 1950 paper had a section&nbsp;
dubbed “Lady Lovelace’s Objection”.&nbsp;

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:42.780
He wrote: “A better variant of&nbsp;
the objection says that a machine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:47.580
can never ‘take us by surprise’. Machines&nbsp;
take me by surprise with great frequency.”

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:53.520
He believed that a machine might be able to learn&nbsp;
and, therefore, be able to generate new thoughts.

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:58.260
His famous test proposed that a computer&nbsp;
can be said to be intelligent if it can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.260 --> 00:07:01.980
convince a person that it’s having a&nbsp;
conversation with another human being.

00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:07.560
But even if a machine could successfully mimic&nbsp;
human thinking, his critics argued it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:12.900
not really be conscious. A machine would lack&nbsp;
comprehension or awareness of what it was saying.

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:16.500
This is still the most debated&nbsp;
topic in cognitive science.

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:22.620
AI does not currently possess the consciousness&nbsp;
or awareness that humans do, or…at least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:24.960
that’s what ChatGPT told me when I asked it!

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:28.860
If only Ada could have seen what&nbsp;
computers are now capable of.

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:35.040
When I uploaded the only known photograph of her&nbsp;
into the AI tool DALL-E, it generated new images.

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:39.360
Ada wanted to enter into a formal&nbsp;
business relationship with Babbage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:41.700
with the aim of building the Analytical Engine.

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:46.080
She offered to use her connections&nbsp;
and persuasive penmanship to gain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:49.740
funding for his engine IF she could&nbsp;
control the business decisions.

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:52.680
She believed Babbage would for sure take the deal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:55.260
writing to her mother: “..that&nbsp;
he will probably yield…”

00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:58.860
Unfortunately for her, Babbage did NOT yield.

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:03.480
Unfortunately for him, he never got the&nbsp;
funding he needed to build his machine.

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:05.100
And he died broke.

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:08.880
Ada Lovelace’s life would spiral downhill.

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:13.860
She struggled with ill health for years,&nbsp;
and her health deteriorated further after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:18.180
she completed her scientific paper, which&nbsp;
was the only paper she ever published.

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:24.060
She took opiates to treat her pain and became&nbsp;
addicted, which led to mood swings and delusions.

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:29.160
She also became addicted to gambling…and&nbsp;
lost thousands of pounds betting on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:32.860
wrong horse in a race and had to&nbsp;
pawn some of her family’s jewels.

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:32.940
Epsom Derby - double-check if she had an&nbsp;
affair with a gambling partner as well

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:37.860
Her finances dwindled despite the fact she had&nbsp;
married the socially prominent William King,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:42.900
who inherited the noble title Earl of&nbsp;
Lovelace, making her Countess of Lovelace.

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:48.600
She had three children and named her first&nbsp;
son Byron, after the father she never knew.

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:54.660
It’s widely believed that Lord Byron also&nbsp;
fathered a child with his own half-sister.

00:08:55.320 --> 00:09:00.480
This didn’t come as a shock to Ada, who wrote to&nbsp;
her mother: “I am not in the least astonished.”

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:05.280
But she didn’t blame her father and instead&nbsp;
called his half-sister “inherently wicked.”

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:11.100
She was fascinated by her father and believed she&nbsp;
had inherited his genius, which she vowed to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:16.560
“to bring out great truths and principles.&nbsp;
I think he has bequeathed this task to me.”

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:20.400
Sadly, her life was cut short.

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:28.680
She suffered from uterine cancer and died&nbsp;
on November 27, 1852 at the age of 36.

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:35.040
At her request, she was buried in a small English&nbsp;
town next to her father, who died at the same age.

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:41.820
In 1991, Science Museum London built Babbage’s&nbsp;
Difference Engine from his original drawings.

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:46.080
As for the analytical engine,&nbsp;
partial sections were built,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:48.480
which Ada had the opportunity to see herself.

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:51.660
However, there are no plans&nbsp;
to recreate it in full.
 &nbsp;

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:56.100
Ada Lovelace is celebrated as a computer pioneer.

00:09:56.100 --> 00:10:00.480
The U.S. Defense Department even named&nbsp;
a programming language after her.

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:03.840
Long before anyone else, Ada Lovelace realized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:08.280
the true potential of computers and&nbsp;
paved the path for our digital age.

